<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Additional Ablation Studies</h3>

<section id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Number of frames for content extraction.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our method is able to extract content information from an arbitrary length of video. We analyzed the result of extracting content with different lengths of input and the results are shown in
<a href="#A2.T5" title="In B.1 Motion Transfer and Generation ‣ Appendix B Additional Results ‣ Bitrate-Controlled Diffusion for Disentangling Motion and Content in Video" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A5</span></a>.
Overall, the overall motion transfer quality is quite robust to the number of content frames with slight differences in terms of error metrics, while using more content reference frames improves the identity preservation and cross-identity motion transfer effect.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Effects of different bitrate.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the main paper, we quantitatively analyzed the effect of different bitrate. We additionally qualitatively visualize the effect in <a href="#A2.F7" title="In B.1 Motion Transfer and Generation ‣ Appendix B Additional Results ‣ Bitrate-Controlled Diffusion for Disentangling Motion and Content in Video" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A7</span></a>: results under a lower bitrate (e.g. 2kbps) leads to degradation of some motion, while results under larger bitrate (<math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math>8kbps) demonstrate worse disentanglement of identity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The usage of bitrate loss.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduced bitrate loss during training to improve bitrate convergence. Ablation studies (see <a href="#A2.T6" title="In B.1 Motion Transfer and Generation ‣ Appendix B Additional Results ‣ Bitrate-Controlled Diffusion for Disentangling Motion and Content in Video" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A6</span></a>) show that removing it degrades performance, confirming its effectiveness.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Evaluation Metrics</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To provide a more thorough evaluation of our method and other baselines on the cross-identity motion transfer task, we proposed additional metrics. These metrics are used together with existing metrics such as CSIM for evaluation results in the main paper. Here we explain the motivation for these additional metrics and their implementation details.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">3D Face Fitting.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All additional metrics rely on a dense reconstruction of 3D face meshes from both target and input videos. For this purpose, we utilize the method described in Wood et al.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> for several reasons.
Firstly, it employs an optimization-based approach using dense landmark observations, ensuring accurate face reconstructions.
In contrast, single-pass feed-forward reconstruction methods like those in Deng et al.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> lack a per-video optimization process and fail to guarantee accurate reconstructions across videos with significant head pose and motion variations.
Secondly, the parametric face model in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite> incorporates detailed blendshape expression bases within a continuous space, facilitating the assessment of expression transfer. Lastly, the dense landmark predictions and the parametric 3D face model from Wood et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite> are trained using high-quality synthetic face images covering a wide range of identities, expressions, head poses, genders, and races, minimizing potential ethical biases and privacy concerns during evaluation. In practice, the average fitting error of the face reconstruction method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite>, as measured by reprojection error in pixel space on our test videos, is less than 1.5 pixels—indicative of high reconstruction quality.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The 3D face reconstruction of a given input video sequence <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> frames is characterized by identity coefficients <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in R^{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in R^{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, per-frame expression coefficients <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi\in R^{n\times m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi\in R^{n\times m}</annotation></semantics></math>, per-frame pose vector (in Euler angle) <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in R^{n\times 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in R^{n\times 3}</annotation></semantics></math>, and per-frame translation vector <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in R^{n\times 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in R^{n\times 3}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The computation of 3D mesh vertices from these coefficients leverages the parametric mesh model described in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite>: <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}(\beta,\theta,\psi,t):R^{c\times m\times 3}\rightarrow R^{v\times 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}(\beta,\theta,\psi,t):R^{c\times m\times 3}\rightarrow R^{v\times 3}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For a comprehensive mathematical explanation, we direct readers to the work of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Shape Error.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The shape error <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is calculated as the mean squared error (MSE) distance between the <span id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">identity meshes</span> of the content reference video and the target video.
An <span id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">identity mesh</span> <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{id}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{id}</annotation></semantics></math> is derived by setting the pose <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, expression <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>, and translation <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> to zero, i.e.,</p>
<table id="A3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M_{identity}=\mathcal{M}(\beta,0,0,0)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M_{identity}=\mathcal{M}(\beta,0,0,0)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle e_{s}=MSE(M^{ref}_{identity},M^{dst}_{identity})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle e_{s}=MSE(M^{ref}_{identity},M^{dst}_{identity})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Thus, the shape error <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> isolates and quantifies the identity discrepancy between the target and the reference input, excluding all other variables.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Motion Error.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Likewise, the motion error <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is determined by calculating the average MSE distance between <span id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">motion meshes</span> of the motion reference video and the target video.
This calculation is achieved by setting the identity coefficients to zero:</p>
<table id="A3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M_{motion}=\mathcal{M}(0,\theta,\phi,t)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M_{motion}=\mathcal{M}(0,\theta,\phi,t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle e_{m}=MSE(M^{ref}_{motion},M^{dst}_{motion})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle e_{m}=MSE(M^{ref}_{motion},M^{dst}_{motion})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">It is important to note that head poses are also incorporated into the motion mesh calculation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Cross Transfer Error.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, the cross transfer error <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is calculated to evaluate the overall quality of the cross-identity motion transfer task by utilizing the fully fitted meshes:</p>
<table id="A3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M_{full}=\mathcal{M}(\beta,\theta,\phi,t)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M_{full}=\mathcal{M}(\beta,\theta,\phi,t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle e_{c}=MSE(M^{ref}_{full},M^{dst}_{full})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle e_{c}=MSE(M^{ref}_{full},M^{dst}_{full})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F8" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<div id="A3.F8.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:159.0pt;"><img src="2509.08376v1/images/csim/appearance_1.png" id="A3.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:220/220;" width="220" height="220" alt="Refer to caption">
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
